;; spec:   types §2.11, §2.12; stdlib design 7 (L30) (method.md rule 3)
;; expect: trap
;; trap:   integer overflow in / at i8
;; `quot` keeps every trap of the division the builtin `/` was, texts
;; included (cases 102 and 103 pin the i64 ones through `quot`): the
;; exact quotient of -128 by -1 is 128, which does not fit an i8, so it
;; traps, as Rust's `/` does, where LLVM's `sdiv` would be undefined
;; behaviour. The narrow width is the point: the check is made at the
;; width of the operands and its message names it. The divisor is
;; computed, so no constant folding can see it.
(defun minus-one (v) (trunc i8 (- (count v) 4)))

(defun main () -> i64
  (sext i64 (quot -128i8 (minus-one [1 2 3]))))
